four years ago this man was paralyzed in  a fall even today his spinal cord is too  badly damaged to transmit signals from  his brain to his limbs and yet he can  walk by using the power of the mind and  a 65 kilo exoskeleton what was performed  was movements of an avatar on-screen and  an exoskeleton in all four limbs and  that's new it's never been done from the  brain controlling all four limbs so  that's exciting for this domain of  research the test subjects name is Tebow  a 28 year old French optician two years  ago back in 2017 the researchers  implanted two sensors in t-bo's skull  either side of the sensory motor cortex  that's the part of the brain which  controls movement the electrodes detect  t-bo's brain signals and then a computer  algorithm processes those signals and  within 350 milliseconds the algorithm  translates the brain's electrical  impulses directly into this mechanical  suit the exoskeleton tebow moves  literally using the power of his mind  first he practiced on controlling a  computer avatar now he's learned to move  all four limbs of the exoskeleton suit  this may look slow and unsteady but in  technological terms it's a massive leap  forward the french scientists behind  this exoskeleton were not available for  interview on friday an official media  launch takes place next week  exoskeletons are not new robotic  mobility assistance has been developing  rapidly in recent years but experts say  that harnessing brain power is still in  its infancy people with very high levels  of tetraplegia are able to control for  example electric wheelchairs by puffing  and sucking with a device now so if this  brain implant is really to transform  that and bring the promise of people by  their thoughts controlling their  mobility in their environment it's got a  long way to go  the road ahead may be long but every  journey begins with a single step  Paul Brennan al-jazeera
 

